@page "/"
@layout MainLayout

<PageTitle>Bdnd - declarative drag and drop for Blazor</PageTitle>

@* The landing page: hero → proof strip → feature grid → code walkthrough → the React DnD mapping →
   persona tiles → FAQ → final call to action. Everything else on the site is a documentation page
   under DocsLayout. The hero's sample is live rather than a picture of one - a drag-and-drop library
   is best introduced by being dragged. *@

<div class="bb-landing">

    @* ============================== Hero ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-hero">
        <div class="bb-hero-copy">
            <span class="bb-hero-eyebrow">
                <span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span>
                Open source · MIT · net8.0 / net9.0 / net10.0
            </span>
            <h1>Drag and drop for Blazor,<br /><span class="bb-hero-accent">the React DnD way.</span></h1>
            <p class="bb-hero-sub">
                Declare what can be dragged and where it can go - <code>BdndDraggable</code>,
                <code>BdndDropTarget</code>, <code>BdndDragLayer</code>, <code>BdndDragHandle</code> - and render
                from the state they hand you. Typed items matched by type, custom drag previews, handles, nesting,
                insertion indicators and native file drops - with the mouse, a finger, a pen or the keyboard, and
                the drag state in C#.
            </p>
            <pre class="bb-code bb-hero-install">dotnet add package Bit.Bdnd</pre>
            <div class="bb-hero-actions">
                <a href="getting-started" class="btn btn-primary bb-btn-lg">Get started →</a>
                <a href="tutorial" class="btn btn-secondary bb-btn-lg">Live demos</a>
                <a class="btn btn-secondary bb-btn-lg" href="@SiteInfo.ProjectUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        @* The sample, framed as a window: the markup on top, and what it renders underneath - live, so
           the first thing a visitor can do here is drag something. *@
        <div class="bb-hero-code">
            <div class="bb-browser">
                <div class="bb-browser-bar">
                    <span class="bb-browser-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
                    <span class="bb-browser-url">Board.razor</span>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BdndDraggable</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Card"</span> Type=<span class="s">"card"</span> Item=<span class="s">"card"</span>&gt;
    …
&lt;/<span class="k">BdndDraggable</span>&gt;

&lt;<span class="k">BdndDropTarget</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Card"</span> Accept=<span class="s">"card"</span> OnDrop=<span class="s">"Archive"</span>&gt;
    …
&lt;/<span class="k">BdndDropTarget</span>&gt;

<span class="k">private void</span> Archive(BdndDropEventArgs&lt;Card&gt; e)
    =&gt; _cards.Remove(e.Item!);</pre>
                <BdndProvider>
                    <div class="home-hero-live">
                        <div class="home-hero-cards">
                            @foreach (var card in _cards)
                            {
                                <BdndDraggable TItem="Card" Type="@ItemTypes.Card" Item="card" Context="drag" @key="card">
                                    <div class="box" style="opacity:@(drag.IsDragging ? "0.4" : "1")">@card.Title</div>
                                </BdndDraggable>
                            }
                            @if (_cards.Count == 0)
                            {
                                <p class="home-hero-done">
                                    All archived.
                                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="Reset">Put them back</button>
                                </p>
                            }
                        </div>

                        <BdndDropTarget TItem="Card" Accept="@ItemTypes.Card" OnDrop="Archive" Context="drop" style="display:contents">
                            <div class="home-hero-bin @(drop.IsOver ? "is-over" : drop.CanDrop ? "is-ready" : null)">
                                @(drop.IsOver ? "Release to archive" : _archived == 0 ? "Drop a card to archive it" : $"{_archived} archived")
                            </div>
                        </BdndDropTarget>
                    </div>
                </BdndProvider>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Proof strip ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-proof">
        <div><strong>React DnD's model</strong><span>items, types, monitors and drop results</span></div>
        <div><strong>Drag state in C#</strong><span>the browser layer only does what C# cannot</span></div>
        <div><strong>Every input</strong><span>mouse, touch, pen and keyboard, with screen-reader announcements</span></div>
        <div><strong>Native OS drags</strong><span>files, text, links and HTML</span></div>
        <div><strong>Every hosting model</strong><span>WebAssembly, Server, Web App and Hybrid</span></div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Feature grid ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Capabilities</p>
        <h2>Everything React DnD does - as Blazor components</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            Where React DnD has hooks, Bdnd has components: a draggable, a drop target, a drag layer and a drag
            handle, each handing its child content the state to render from. The demo ports React DnD's own
            examples, so every one of them can be opened side by side.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-grid">
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Sources and targets</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>BdndDraggable</code> is <code>useDrag</code> and <code>BdndDropTarget</code> is
                    <code>useDrop</code>: <code>Type</code> and <code>Item</code> on one, <code>Accept</code> and
                    <code>OnDrop</code> on the other, and <code>IsDragging</code>, <code>IsOver</code> and
                    <code>CanDrop</code> to render from.
                </p>
                <a href="dustbin/single" class="btn btn-outline">Single target →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Typed items, gated drops</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Any C# object is an item, matched by its type string. <code>CanDropPredicate</code> narrows a
                    target further from the item itself - a chessboard square takes only a legal knight move.
                </p>
                <a href="tutorial" class="btn btn-outline">Chessboard tutorial →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Custom previews and handles</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    <code>UseCustomDragLayer</code> hides the browser's ghost and a <code>BdndDragLayer</code> draws
                    your own from the live offsets; <code>UseHandle</code> starts a drag only from a
                    <code>BdndDragHandle</code>.
                </p>
                <a href="drag-around/custom-layer" class="btn btn-outline">Custom drag layer →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> Sortable lists</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Cards that are each a draggable and a drop target, reordered from <code>OnHover</code> as the
                    dragged card passes their middle - React DnD's algorithm, without the flicker.
                </p>
                <a href="sortable/simple" class="btn btn-outline">Sortable →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Drop indicators</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    A target with <code>Edges</code> reports the edge a drag is closest to - the line that shows
                    where the item lands - and <code>BdndList</code> turns it into the move, within a list or across
                    lists, in grids, trees and virtualized lists.
                </p>
                <a href="sortable/indicators" class="btn btn-outline">Drop indicators →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Nesting</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Nested sources drag the innermost one; nested targets report <code>IsOver</code> for every
                    target under the pointer and <code>IsOverShallow</code> for the innermost, and drops reach them
                    innermost first.
                </p>
                <a href="nesting/drop-targets" class="btn btn-outline">Nested drop targets →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Native drags</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Accept files from the desktop - streaming their contents, opening up folders, limited to the
                    types and count you allow - and text, links and HTML from any page, with
                    <code>BdndNativeTypes</code> and <code>OnNativeDrop</code>.
                </p>
                <a href="other/native-files" class="btn btn-outline">Native files →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Touch and pen</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Most touch browsers raise no HTML5 drag events, so touch and pen drag through pointer events: a
                    press-and-hold that leaves swipes to scroll, a copy that follows the finger and auto-scrolling.
                    <code>PointerDrag="All"</code> drags the mouse the same way.
                </p>
                <a href="customize/pointer" class="btn btn-outline">Touch and pointer drags →</a>
            </section>
            <section class="bb-card">
                <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Keyboard and screen readers</h3>
                <p class="bb-card-desc">
                    Every draggable can be picked up with <kbd>Space</kbd>, moved between the targets that can take
                    it with the arrow keys and dropped with <kbd>Space</kbd>, while a live region tells screen
                    readers what happens - in words you can translate.
                </p>
                <a href="accessibility/keyboard" class="btn btn-outline">Keyboard dragging →</a>
            </section>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Code walkthrough ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">In practice</p>
        <h2>See it in code</h2>
        <div class="bb-walkthrough">
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">01</span>
                    <h3>Wrap a region in a provider</h3>
                    <p>
                        One service registration, then a <code>BdndProvider</code> around whatever drags and drops.
                        Each provider creates the manager its components share - there is nothing to configure.
                    </p>
                    <a href="getting-started">Getting started →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">builder.Services.AddBitBdndServices();   <span class="c">// Program.cs</span>

&lt;<span class="k">BdndProvider</span>&gt;
    …draggables and drop targets…
&lt;/<span class="k">BdndProvider</span>&gt;</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">02</span>
                    <h3>Declare a source and a target</h3>
                    <p>
                        A draggable carries an item of a type; a target accepts the types it lists. Both hand their
                        content the collected state, so the hover and the dragging look are one expression each.
                    </p>
                    <a href="dustbin/multiple">Multiple targets →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BdndDraggable</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Box"</span> Type=<span class="s">"box"</span> Item=<span class="s">"box"</span> Context=<span class="s">"drag"</span>&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">div</span> style=<span class="s">"opacity: @@(drag.IsDragging ? 0.4 : 1)"</span>&gt;@@box.Name&lt;/<span class="k">div</span>&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">BdndDraggable</span>&gt;

&lt;<span class="k">BdndDropTarget</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Box"</span> AcceptTypes=<span class="s">"@@(["box", "crate"])"</span>
                OnDrop=<span class="s">"Store"</span> Context=<span class="s">"drop"</span>&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">div</span> class=<span class="s">"bin @@(drop.CanDrop ? "ready" : "")"</span>&gt;Drop here&lt;/<span class="k">div</span>&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">BdndDropTarget</span>&gt;</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-walkthrough-row">
                <div class="bb-walkthrough-copy">
                    <span class="bb-step">03</span>
                    <h3>Read the outcome</h3>
                    <p>
                        A target can publish a result from <code>OnDrop</code>; the source's <code>OnEnd</code>
                        receives it with <code>DidDrop</code>, which tells a drop from a cancel - and runs even if
                        the drop re-rendered the source away.
                    </p>
                    <a href="dustbin/copy-or-move">Copy or move →</a>
                </div>
                <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">BdndDraggable</span> TItem=<span class="s">"Box"</span> Type=<span class="s">"box"</span> Item=<span class="s">"box"</span> OnEnd=<span class="s">"Ended"</span>&gt;…&lt;/<span class="k">BdndDraggable</span>&gt;

<span class="k">private void</span> Ended(BdndDragEndContext&lt;Box&gt; e)
{
    <span class="k">if</span> (e.DidDrop) _palette.Remove(e.Item!);   <span class="c">// e.DropResult: what the target set</span>
}</pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ============================== Comparison ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Comparison</p>
        <h2>Coming from React DnD?</h2>
        <p class="bb-section-sub">
            The same concepts under the names a Blazor component gives them. There are no connector refs to
            attach - a component's host element is the source or the target - and native drops have a callback of
            their own.
        </p>
        <div class="bb-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="React DnD compared with Bit.Bdnd">
            <table class="bb-table bb-compare">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Concern</th><th>React DnD</th><th>Bit.Bdnd</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>The context</td><td><code>&lt;DndProvider backend={HTML5Backend}&gt;</code></td><td><code>&lt;BdndProvider&gt;</code>, the HTML5 backend built in</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>A drag source</td><td><code>useDrag({ type, item })</code></td><td><code>&lt;BdndDraggable Type Item&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>A drop target</td><td><code>useDrop({ accept, drop })</code></td><td><code>&lt;BdndDropTarget Accept OnDrop&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Only the innermost target</td><td><code>isOver({ shallow: true })</code></td><td><code>IsOverShallow</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Your own preview</td><td><code>getEmptyImage()</code> and <code>useDragLayer</code></td><td><code>UseCustomDragLayer</code> and <code>&lt;BdndDragLayer&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Files from the desktop</td><td><code>NativeTypes.FILE</code> in <code>drop</code></td><td><code>BdndNativeTypes.File</code> in <code>OnNativeDrop</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Touch screens</td><td><code>TouchBackend</code>, a backend of its own</td><td>Built in: touch and pen drag through pointer events (<code>PointerDrag</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>The keyboard</td><td>Not built in</td><td>Built in: <kbd>Space</kbd>, the arrow keys, <kbd>Space</kbd> - announced to screen readers</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>

