    @* ============================== FAQ ============================== *@

    <section class="bb-section">
        <p class="bb-overline">Questions</p>
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <div class="bb-faq">
            <details>
                <summary>Is this React DnD running through JavaScript interop?</summary>
                <p>
                    No. The drag state - the item, its type, the source, the offsets, every target's hover and the
                    drop result - lives in a C# manager shared by the components of a <code>BdndProvider</code>. A
                    small script module does only what C# cannot: configure the native drag while
                    <code>dragstart</code> is being dispatched, follow the pointer, track hover and read native drops.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Which render modes does it work in?</summary>
                <p>
                    Any interactive one, on .NET 8, 9 and 10: WebAssembly is the best fit, since every
                    <code>dragover</code> is handled without a network round trip; Server, Auto and Hybrid work the
                    same, a round trip later - the <a href="hosting/server-check">Blazor Server check</a> page drags
                    over a SignalR circuit. Prerendered markup shows the idle state and starts working once the page
                    is interactive.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Does it work on touch screens?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes. Most touch browsers never turn a touch into an HTML5 drag, so Bdnd drags touch and pen
                    through pointer events of its own: a finger that rests on a draggable for a moment
                    (<code>TouchDelay</code>, 250 ms) picks it up, so a swipe across a list still scrolls; a copy of the
                    item follows the finger, and the container under it scrolls near its edges. The mouse keeps the
                    browser's own drag and drop - or set <code>PointerDrag="All"</code> for the same pointer drag
                    everywhere.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Can it be used without a mouse?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes. Every draggable is focusable and can be picked up with <kbd>Space</kbd> or
                    <kbd>Enter</kbd>, moved between the drop targets that can take it with the arrow keys, and dropped
                    with <kbd>Space</kbd> or cancelled with <kbd>Escape</kbd>. A live region tells screen readers what
                    happens, in texts you can reword or translate through <code>BdndAnnouncements</code>.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Can I read the contents of a dropped file?</summary>
                <p>
                    Yes. Every dropped file is a <code>BdndNativeFile</code> - its name, size, MIME type and
                    last-modified date - and <code>OpenReadStreamAsync</code> streams its contents from the browser,
                    over the circuit on Blazor Server, up to the size you allow. A dropped folder opens up into the
                    files inside it with <code>ExpandDirectories</code>, and <code>AcceptFileTypes</code> and
                    <code>MaxFiles</code> limit what a drop hands over.
                </p>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary>Is there a stylesheet to include?</summary>
                <p>
                    No. Bdnd draws nothing of its own - what is dragged and where it lands look the way your markup
                    says - and its script module is imported by the components the first time they need it.
                </p>
            </details>
        </div>
    </section>

